Task: Implement Redis-backed CartStore (Phase 2 of redis-cart-model_20261002)

Summary: Added redis4cats (redis4cats-effects/log4cats) + testcontainers-scala-redis
(swapped out for the now-unused testcontainers-scala-postgresql) to build.sbt.
Implemented CartStore.redis[F], backing the Phase 1 line-item algebra with a Redis
Hash per cart (key `cart:<id>`, fields = sku -> stringified quantity, plus two
reserved metadata fields `__created_at`/`__updated_at`). addItem/removeItem/
setQuantity use HINCRBY/HDEL/HSET - atomic against concurrent same-cart writes - via
a shared `mutateIfExists` helper that checks existence first (so HINCRBY can't
auto-create a hash for a cart that was never created, which is Redis's own default
behavior and would otherwise violate "None if the cart doesn't exist"), then refreshes
the cart's TTL and the updatedAt field on every write. Instrumented with the same
db.client.operation.duration histogram pattern the Postgres store used, with
db.system="redis". Added a standalone RedisConfig(uri, ttlSeconds) to
CartServiceConfig.scala - mirrors order-service's own RedisConfig shape exactly
(deviation: the approved plan said "RedisConfig (host, port)", but testcontainers-
scala-redis's RedisContainer only exposes a redisUri, and redis4cats connects via a
URI string - uri+ttlSeconds matches the already-working order-service precedent in
this same codebase with zero added plumbing) - not yet wired into the aggregate
CartServiceConfig (that's Phase 3).

Deliberately out of scope here (per spec): product snapshotting, catalog-service
calls, and any CartRoutes/Main.scala wiring - this phase only proves the store
against a real Redis via CartStoreRedisSuite.

Files created/modified:
- src/main/scala/cartservice/CartStore.scala (added CartStore.redis + helpers)
- src/main/scala/cartservice/CartServiceConfig.scala (added standalone RedisConfig)
- build.sbt (redis4cats deps added; testcontainers-scala-postgresql swapped for
  testcontainers-scala-redis)
- src/test/scala/cartservice/CartStoreRedisSuite.scala (new - 17 tests, including a
  TTL assertion opening a direct redis4cats connection, and a metrics assertion)

Why: spec requires a Redis-backed implementation of the line-item algebra, atomic
against concurrent same-cart mutations, with session TTL - the whole reason this
track exists (system-design.md pins cart-service as Redis-only).

